**New evidence**
- Accepted claims below.
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"World Bank Supports Primary Healthcare and Universal Coverage to Strengthen Human Capital in Pakistan","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-07","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/06/world-bank-supports-primary-healthcare-and-universal-coverage-to-strengthen-human-capital-in-pakistan","claim":"The World Bank approved $258 million for the National Health Support Program to strengthen primary health care and accelerate universal health coverage in Pakistan, with emphasis on children and women during pregnancy and childbirth.","locator":"lines 144-146","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank press release with program amount, purpose, and target groups; strong for policy design but not proof of rollout.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published during the 2022 target year and directly relevant to Shehbaz Sharif's premiership.","ruler_attribution":"Shared attribution: this was a federal health-financing action during Sharif's tenure, but the source does not show a personal PM directive or completed rollout in 2022.","contrary_evidence":["Approval alone does not prove full implementation or measurable 2022 outcome.","Health-system delivery remained constrained by preexisting gaps and later flood disruption."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Towards sustainable financing for PHC and UHC: The piloting of Essential Package of Health Services in Pakistan","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-05-18","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/feature-stories/detail/bringing-support-phc-sustainable-health-financing-together-pakistan","claim":"Pakistan approved a limited national Essential Package of Health Services for immediate district-level implementation at $12.98 per person per year.","locator":"line 219","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO feature story with a specific national package and implementation cost; reliable for policy design, not independent outcome measurement.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within the 2022 target year.","ruler_attribution":"Shared attribution through the Ministry of Health during Sharif's tenure; the page shows a national implementation package but not a PM personally authored reform.","contrary_evidence":["The package was limited and depended on broader fiscal space for full implementation.","It documents design rather than demonstrated nationwide delivery in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"UNICEF Geneva Palais briefing note on the impact of the Pakistan floods on children and families and UNICEF's response","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-09-02","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/unicef-geneva-palais-briefing-note-impact-pakistan-floods-children-and-families-and","claim":"UNICEF said it had already delivered more than $2 million in emergency services and supplies, including drinking water, hygiene kits, medicines, vaccines, therapeutic food for children and pregnant and lactating women, and mosquito nets, as part of the government-led flood response.","locator":"lines 203-206","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF briefing note with concrete response items, quantities, and timing; strong for humanitarian response documentation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency and target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect but real: this is UNICEF's response within a government-led flash appeal, so it supports Sharif-era crisis management but not a personal order for each supply item.","contrary_evidence":["This is emergency relief in response to catastrophic damage, not evidence of durable system improvement by itself.","The note also describes severe service disruption and high child vulnerability."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Schools for more than 2 million children in Pakistan remain inaccessible due to devastating floods","publisher":"UNICEF","publication_date":"2022-11-03","url":"https://www.unicef.org/press-releases/schools-more-2-million-children-pakistan-remain-inaccessible-due-devastating-floods","claim":"More than 2 million children remained out of school after floods damaged or destroyed nearly 27,000 schools, and UNICEF established more than 500 temporary learning centres with education supplies.","locator":"lines 194 and 200","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF press release with clear counts and specific response measures; strong for education disruption and mitigation.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly relevant to the 2022 flood shock and the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Mainly indirect: the school damage is disaster context, while the temporary learning centres reflect aid and government-supported recovery rather than a pure PM-ordered domestic education reform.","contrary_evidence":["The source mainly documents disruption and humanitarian mitigation, not a completed education-system turnaround.","The scale of school damage limits any inference of overall improvement in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan: World Bank Approves $1.692 Billion for 5 Projects to Support the People of Sindh in the Flood-Affected Areas","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/19/pakistan-world-bank-approves-1-692-billion-for-5-projects-to-support-the-people-of-sindh-in-the-flood-affected-areas","claim":"The World Bank approved $1.692 billion for five projects in flood-affected Sindh, including projects that support rehabilitation and housing reconstruction, restore crop production for vulnerable communities, and expand health services for mothers and children.","locator":"lines 55 and 150-158","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank financing decision with explicit sectoral allocation and target groups.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Issued in 2022 and tied to the flood response during Sharif's premiership.","ruler_attribution":"Shared attribution: Sharif's government was the counterpart, but the projects are World Bank-financed and implemented with provincial and partner systems rather than personally ordered by the PM in the cited text.","contrary_evidence":["The package responds to extraordinary flood losses and does not show pre-flood structural improvement.","Financing approval does not prove completed delivery in 2022."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Factsheet: Strengthening Social Protection Delivery System in Sindh","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-19","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/factsheet/2022/12/19/factsheet-strengthening-social-protection-delivery-system-in-sindh","claim":"The project will strengthen Sindh's social protection delivery system and promote maternal and newborn child health through conditional cash transfers, prioritizing poor and vulnerable women and children and directly benefiting 1.3 million mothers and their children.","locator":"lines 53 and 140-144","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank factsheet with explicit target population, mechanism, and delivery-system details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in the target year and directly tied to flood recovery and social protection.","ruler_attribution":"Shared attribution only: the project fits the Sharif-era federal/provincial recovery environment, but the factsheet does not show personal PM implementation control.","contrary_evidence":["The project is province-specific and partner-dependent, so attribution to the federal PM is partial.","The factsheet describes planned benefits and system strengthening rather than measured outcomes by year-end."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WFP steps up humanitarian support to government relief efforts as Pakistan reels from devastating floods","publisher":"World Food Programme","publication_date":"2022-08-30","url":"https://www.wfp.org/news/wfp-steps-humanitarian-support-government-relief-efforts-pakistan-reels-devastating-floods","claim":"WFP said it was stepping up support to the government relief effort and aimed to reach up to 1 million people with food, nutrition, and livelihoods assistance, including 31,000 children and 28,000 pregnant and breastfeeding women with specialized nutritious foods.","locator":"lines 257 and 271-272","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WFP operational update with concrete beneficiary counts and categories.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect and shared: the government was leading the national emergency response, while WFP was the external operational partner.","contrary_evidence":["This is humanitarian relief in response to disaster, not evidence of structural welfare reform.","The source shows severe access constraints and worsening humanitarian conditions."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Director-General's remarks at the OCHA briefing on the emergency flood response in Pakistan - 4 October 2022","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-10-04","url":"https://www.who.int/news-room/speeches/item/who-director-general-s-remarks-at-the-ocha-briefing-on-the-emergency-flood-response-in-pakistan---4-october-2022","claim":"WHO said the Government of Pakistan had delivered an impressive rescue and first-phase relief operation and that decisive early warning and immediate response actions by the government and local communities helped save lives, while urgent action was still needed to prevent disease and severe acute malnutrition.","locator":"lines 236-250","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO leadership remarks, useful for response assessment though still a humanitarian-and-coordination perspective rather than an audit.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency.","ruler_attribution":"Relatively strong attribution for crisis management because the WHO head explicitly credits government early warning and response, though not every implementation detail is attributable to Sharif personally.","contrary_evidence":["WHO also described a looming public health disaster and disrupted health services.","The praise is for emergency response, not for long-run social development."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Impact in 2022 - Pakistan","publisher":"World Health Organization","publication_date":"2022-10-05","url":"https://www.who.int/emergencies/funding/health-emergency-appeals/2022/impact-in-2022/pakistan","claim":"WHO said it carried out a measles-rubella vaccination campaign in 38 districts and vaccinated 1.8 million children aged six months to 59 months to help control transmission after the floods.","locator":"lines 217-219","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct WHO program report with specific campaign scope and beneficiary count.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Within the 2022 flood-response window.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect: the campaign is a WHO response supporting Pakistan's flood-affected health system, not a PM-ordered vaccination directive, but it does reflect the government's crisis environment and cooperation.","contrary_evidence":["The same page says about 10% of health facilities were damaged or destroyed and more than eight million people needed urgent health assistance.","This is a remediation response to severe disruption, not a clean baseline success story."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Rebuilding the Livelihoods of Flood-Affected Rural Communities","publisher":"Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations","publication_date":"unknown","url":"https://www.fao.org/pakistan/our-work/success-stories/project-success-stories/rebuilding-the-livelihoods-of-flood-affected-rural-communities/en/","claim":"FAO provided emergency agricultural assistance to 40,000 flood-affected families, targeting communities in Balochistan and Sindh and focusing especially on women-headed households to restore livelihoods and improve food security.","locator":"lines 18-20","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"FAO success story with specific beneficiary counts, geography, and intervention details; publication date is not explicit on the page so the date is marked unknown.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Retrospective on the 2022 floods and directly relevant to the target year.","ruler_attribution":"Indirect and shared: this is partner-led recovery work in a Sharif-era flood context, not direct PM execution.","contrary_evidence":["This is humanitarian livelihood repair after disaster rather than evidence of ordinary-year social progress.","The source is retrospective and does not itself prove federal policy quality."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Readout from World Bank Group President David Malpass's Meeting with Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif of Pakistan","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-09-21","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/speech/2022/09/21/readout-from-world-bank-group-president-david-malpass-s-meeting-with-prime-minister-shehbaz-sharif-of-pakistan","claim":"After meeting Prime Minister Shehbaz Sharif, the World Bank said it would provide immediate financing to support social protection for people most affected by flooding and rebuild roads, dams, houses, schools, and health centers damaged by the floods.","locator":"lines 435-437","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Direct World Bank readout of a PM meeting, with concrete financing and reconstruction targets.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly within the 2022 flood emergency and Sharif's term.","ruler_attribution":"Moderate: the page anchors the commitment in a meeting with Sharif, but the actual financing and implementation belong to the World Bank and broader government system.","contrary_evidence":["This is a financing commitment rather than completed reconstruction.","The source reflects flood recovery under severe disaster pressure, not a normal welfare baseline."],"lenses":["6B.6","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Prime Minister of Pakistan Muhammad Shehbaz Sharif launches the Resilient, Recovery, Rehabilitation and Reconstruction Framework (4RF) for Pakistan Floods","publisher":"Press Information Department, Government of Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-12-21","url":"https://pid.gov.pk/site/press_detail/21692","claim":"Shehbaz Sharif launched the 4RF for Pakistan Floods 2022, describing it as a short-, medium-, and long-term climate-resilient action plan for rehabilitation and reconstruction after the floods.","locator":"lines 46-50","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"Official government release with explicit PM action and policy framing, but it is self-reporting and not independently audited.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly in the target year and tied to the 2022 flood recovery process.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: the PM is explicitly named as the launcher and the document says he directed dissemination and stakeholder engagement.","contrary_evidence":["The release is about framework launch, not verified implementation or welfare outcomes.","It is a government self-report and needs corroboration from later delivery evidence."],"lenses":["6B.5","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Pakistan Maternal Nutrition Strategy 2022-27","publisher":"UNICEF Pakistan","publication_date":"2022-05","url":"https://www.unicef.org/pakistan/reports/pakistan-maternal-nutrition-strategy-2022-27","claim":"A countrywide consultation produced a maternal nutrition strategy to protect and promote diets, practices, and services for women in Pakistan, with evidence-based and equity-based nutrition programmes focused on pregnancy, breastfeeding, and preventing malnutrition.","locator":"lines 56-66","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Joint UNICEF and Government of Pakistan strategy document with clear policy aims and publication date in the target year.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in the target year and explicitly spans 2022-27.","ruler_attribution":"Shared attribution: the strategy was formulated with the Government of Pakistan, but the page does not show a personal PM directive or a completed national rollout by year-end.","contrary_evidence":["A strategy document is not the same as delivered services or improved outcomes in 2022.","The page shows planning and consultation rather than independent implementation results."],"lenses":["6B.1","6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
- I screened roughly 30 documents across World Bank, WHO, UNICEF, WFP, FAO, IMF, the Government of Pakistan, and a few later retrospective pieces to separate 2022 action from later commentary.
- I kept 13 new source-claim units from 12 distinct URLs and reused 8 prior evidence items that genuinely fit this chapter.
- I rejected duplicate flood-response writeups from UNICEF, WFP, and World Bank when they repeated the same beneficiary counts or relief package without adding a new materially distinct claim.
- I rejected out-of-period follow-ups from 2023-2026 unless they directly described 2022 actions and added something materially new.
- I rejected baseline-only documents, such as generic Pakistan profile pages or macro indicators, when they did not show a ruler-linked welfare action.
- I treated local structured priors as context only: Pakistan’s 2022 baseline shows HDI 0.54, life expectancy 66.4, under-5 mortality 50.5, and BCG/DTP3 immunization at 95/85, but those figures were not used as independent evidence of Sharif’s action.
- A few government pages had cache/fetch issues during lookup; I did not rely on them when I could use accessible official or IGO sources instead.

**Remaining gaps**
- The biggest gap is ordinary-year, non-flood social policy under Sharif in 2022. Most strong evidence is emergency response after the floods, not steady-state welfare expansion.
- I still lack direct household-survey or administrative-outcome evidence showing how much 2022 decisions improved poverty, schooling, sanitation, nutrition, or health access by year-end.
- Attribution remains partly shared with provinces, the World Bank, UNICEF, WHO, WFP, and FAO. More work could sharpen what Sharif personally directed versus what his government inherited or coordinated.
- I do not yet have a clean, direct 2022 source on whether social protection and cash transfers were fully depoliticized and consistently reached the poorest groups across provinces.
- More search would help most on implementation audits, provincial delivery records, and 2022 budget execution for health, education, WASH, and social protection.
